point Long Bridge#Trestle fr. Sunnyside to TrestleCr.#{1}":[322,323],"##The Sandpoint Long Bridge#What's in a Name":[324,327],"##The Sandpoint Long Bridge#What's in a Name#{1}":[326,327],"##The Sandpoint Long Bridge#The Whitaker House":[328,332],"##The Sandpoint Long Bridge#The Whitaker House#{1}":[330,332]},"outlinks":[{"title":"Bob Gunter","target":"Bob Gunter","line":5},{"title":"Sandpoint and Surrounding Communities History","target":"https://www.sandpoint.com/community/images/Sandpoint-Idaho-History-page_xlarge.jpg \"Sandpoint and Surrounding Communities History\"","line":10,"embedded":true},{"title":"Fall Activities in Sandpoint","target":"https://www.sandpoint.com/images/foot-icon-fall.png \"Fall Activities in Sandpoint\"","line":332,"embedded":true},{"title":"Spring Activities in Sandpoint","target":"https://www.sandpoint.com/images/foot-icon-spring.png \"Spring Activities in Sandpoint\"","line":332,"embedded":true},{"title":"Summer Activities in Sandpoint","target":"https://www.sandpoint.com/images/foot-icon-summer.png \"Summer Activities in Sandpoint\"","line":332,"embedded":true},{"title":"Winter Activities in Sandpoint","target":"https://www.sandpoint.com/images/foot-icon-winter.png \"Winter Activities in Sandpoint\"","line":332,"embedded":true},{"title":"Skiing Sandpoint, Idaho - Outdoor Activities in Sandpoint - Watersports on Lake Pend Oreille","target":"https://www.sandpoint.com/images/Sandpoint-Recreation_footer-img.jpg \"Skiing Sandpoint, Idaho - Outdoor Activities in Sandpoint - Watersports on Lake Pend Oreille\"","line":332,"embedded":true}],"metadata":{"title":"The Sandpoint Long Bridge - Sandpoint and North Idaho History Guide","source":"https://www.sandpoint.com/community/long-bridge.php","author":["[[Bob Gunter]]"],"published":"11:21 am PDT","created":"2026-07-13","description":"On 26 May 1908 the first pilings for the first bridge connecting Sagle, Idaho with Sandpoint, Idaho were driven. When completed the bridge was supported by 1,540 pilings and covered a distance of almost two miles. Near the center was a steel lift which allowed the steam boat traffic to pass underneath. After numerous problems the bridge was completed and ready for use on March 11, 1910 and was advertised as the longest wooden bridge in the world. When completed the bridge was about 200 feet short of two miles long. It served the area for many years until in the 1930's it was torn down to make room for a new wooden bridge."},"task_lines":[],"tasks":{},"codeblock_ranges":[]},"smart_blocks:11_21 am PDT - The Sandpoint Long Bridge - Sandpoint and North Idaho History Guide.md#---frontmatter---": {"path":null,"last_embed":{"hash":null},"embeddings":{},"text":null,"length":0,"last_read":{"hash":"8w927h","at":1785795002173},"key":"11_21 am PDT - The Sandpoint Long Bridge - Sandpoint and North Idaho History Guide.md#---frontmatter---","lines":[1,9],"size":864,"outlinks":[{"title":"Bob Gunter","target":"Bob Gunter","line":5}],"class_name":"SmartBlock"},
